  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed method iteratively identifies and mitigates the worst-case overlap by adjusting h to reduce contributions from the most problematic shifts. This avoids the computational overhead of solving a large non-convex problem directly, instead making local improvements that cumulatively lower the maximum overlap.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a vector of n_points elements, each constrained to [0,1], with \u2211h = n_points / 2. The overlap integral C is computed as the maximum cross-correlation between h and 1 - h, scaled by dx. The objective is to minimize C.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe algorithm proceeds as follows:\na. Initialization:\n   1. Start with the initial_h_values if provided, else initialize h to 0.5.\n   2. Clip h to [0,1] and normalize to ensure \u2211h = n_points / 2.\nb. Main loop:\n   i. Compute cross-correlation c = np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode='full').\n   ii. Identify the shift(s) where c is maximum.\n   iii. For each such shift k:\n        - Adjust h[i] and h[i + k] to reduce their product h[i]*(1 - h[i + k]).\n   iv. Clip h to [0,1] and renormalize to \u2211h = n_points / 2.\n   v. Track the best found h and corresponding C.\nc. Termination:\n   - Stop after budget_s or no improvement for several iterations.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Step sizes: Start small, increase if progress is steady.\n- Fallback: Return initial_h_values if no progress.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Runtime dominated by cross-correlation and updates.\n- Designed to handle numerical precision and constraints.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure h is feasible and C computed correctly.",
